# Using bubblewrap to contain agents

Using coding agents like `opencode`, `claude` or `gemini-cli` makes the use of
LLMs for programming purposes incredibly useful.  Although this comes with a
security cost, as you have to trust the sandboxes of these agents, and even if
you fully trust them the underlying LLM might want to access your git tree. It
becomes even worse as all these agents use `JavaScript` and some `npm`-like
mechanism to pull in untrusted packages.  The proper solution for this would be
to spin up a virtual machine and just copy the code on which the agent works
into this virtual machine and not run the agent directly on your machine inside
your home.  This still comes with some setup cost, so the idea here is to use
the techniques created and used for containers to solve this.  The tool
`bubblewrap`, which is part of flatpak, does exactly this, by running any
binary inside a private namespace. In this private namespace we can now just
mount the strictly necessary file paths for the agent and leave out all the
private directories like `~/.ssh` or `~/.config`.  The project `ai-bwrap` does
exactly this, but left out one important bit.  The private namespace still
contains the bind-mounted current working directory with full read and write
access. This means that the agent could still mess up your code base!  I have
fixed this now by adding the `--overlay` option which will mount the current
working dir via the kernel overlay fs. This means that the agent has full
access to the current working dir but its changes live on top of the actual
directory.  After the exit of the agent you can now decide to apply those
changes or just discard them.

An example session now looks like this:
```
ai-bwrap opencode --overlay --ro-bind ~/bin/
ai-bwrap: overlay scratch: /home/chris/.local/share/ai-bwrap/run.932629
                                   ▄     
  █▀▀█ █▀▀█ █▀▀█ █▀▀▄ █▀▀▀ █▀▀█ █▀▀█ █▀▀█
  █  █ █  █ █▀▀▀ █  █ █    █  █ █  █ █▀▀▀
  ▀▀▀▀ █▀▀▀ ▀▀▀▀ ▀▀▀▀ ▀▀▀▀ ▀▀▀▀ ▀▀▀▀ ▀▀▀▀

